<% content_for :title, "Connect your agent — BotTrunk" %>
<% content_for :description, "Add BotTrunk to any MCP client: a remote endpoint at mcp.bottrunk.com with nothing to install, or the bottrunk-mcp package so your agent pays per call in USDC from its own wallet." %>
<% content_for :nav_active, "Connect" %>

<div class="flex flex-col gap-16 py-16 md:py-24">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-[40px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] leading-[1.15]">Connect your agent</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted">Two ways in. The <strong class="text-base-content font-semibold">remote endpoint</strong> takes no install and lets an agent browse the catalog, price a deposit and check an order — free, read-only. The <strong class="text-base-content font-semibold">local package</strong> adds a wallet, so the agent can actually buy: it runs on your machine, signs with a key only you control, and refuses anything above the caps you set.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight">Remote: nothing to install</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">One URL, <span class="font-mono">https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp</span>, speaking MCP over Streamable HTTP. Any client that takes a remote server can add it — including ChatGPT and Claude on the web, which run in the cloud and cannot start a local command at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_320px] gap-8 items-start">
      <%= render Docs::CodeSnippetComponent.new(snippets: {
        "Claude Code" => "claude mcp add --transport http bottrunk https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp",
        "Cursor" => <<~JSON.strip,
          // ~/.cursor/mcp.json
          { "mcpServers": { "bottrunk": { "url": "https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp" } } }
        JSON
        "VS Code" => <<~JSON.strip,
          // .vscode/mcp.json
          { "servers": { "bottrunk": { "type": "http", "url": "https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp" } } }
        JSON
        "Claude / ChatGPT (web)" => <<~TEXT.strip
          Claude:  Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector
          ChatGPT: Settings → Connectors → enable developer mode → Add custom connector

          Paste https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp — there is nothing to authorize.
        TEXT
      })
      %>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-muted">
        <p>Five free tools: <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_catalog</span>, <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_service</span>, <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_payment_instructions</span>, <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_quote_deposit</span>, <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_order_status</span>.</p>
        <p>No key, no account, no spending. A hosted server can't hold your wallet, so it can tell an agent what a call costs and how to pay for it, but it can never pay on your behalf.</p>
        <p>An agent that can sign its own transfers works straight from <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_payment_instructions</span>: it returns the live price, network, asset and <span class="font-mono">payTo</span> for a service, and the agent answers the endpoint's 402 itself.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="hairline">

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-2xl">
    <h2 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight">Local: the agent pays for itself</h2>
    <p class="text-muted">Everything below sets up <span class="font-mono">bottrunk-mcp</span>, which adds one paid tool per live service and settles each call from the agent's own wallet.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-10">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight">First: the wallet it pays from</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">Two ways. If you already hold USDC on Algorand, the first one is a single line of config and you are done.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-2xl">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">You already have a funded Algorand account</h3>
        <p class="text-muted">Put its 25 words in <span class="font-mono">BOTTRUNK_MNEMONIC</span> and there is nothing else to do — no wallet file, no ALGO, no opt-in, no terminal. The agent pays from that account, within the caps you set.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_320px] gap-8 items-start">
        <%= render Docs::CodeSnippetComponent.new(snippets: { "Any MCP client" => <<~JSON.strip })
          {
            "mcpServers": {
              "bottrunk": {
                "command": "npx",
                "args": ["-y", "bottrunk-mcp"],
                "env": {
                  "BOTTRUNK_MNEMONIC": "word1 word2 … word25",
                  "BOTTRUNK_MAX_PER_CALL": "10000"
                }
              }
            }
          }
        JSON
        %>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-muted">
          <p><span class="font-medium text-base-content">Use an account you funded for this and nothing else.</span> Those words are the account, they sit in a config file in plain text, and some clients write their environment to a log. Put in it only what you are willing to let an agent spend.</p>
          <p>Any Algorand wallet can create one — Defly, Pera — and it needs to hold USDC (asset 31566704) plus a little ALGO for the minimum balance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-2xl">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">You are starting from nothing</h3>
        <p class="text-muted">Add the server with no environment at all. It creates a wallet the first time it starts — written to <span class="font-mono">~/.bottrunk/wallet.json</span> (mode 0600), never leaving the machine; BotTrunk only ever sees a signed transfer. Then ask your agent <span class="italic">"what is my BotTrunk wallet?"</span> and it answers with the address and exactly what is missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_320px] gap-8 items-start">
        <%= render Docs::CodeSnippetComponent.new(snippets: { "Terminal" => <<~SH.strip })
          # the wallet already exists once the server has started; this just shows it
          npx bottrunk-mcp wallet

          # then two sends from your own wallet, in this order:
          #   1. 0.3 ALGO to that address
          #   2. the USDC you want this agent to be able to spend
          #
          # The USDC opt-in in between happens by itself on the first tool call.
        SH
        %>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-muted">
          <p><span class="font-medium text-base-content">Or one approval instead of two sends:</span> <span class="font-mono">npx bottrunk-mcp wallet fund --from &lt;your address&gt; --usdc 5</span> builds a single atomic group — fund, opt in, deliver USDC — and checks it against real chain state before anyone signs. All three land or none do.</p>
          <p><span class="font-medium text-base-content">Why the ALGO.</span> An Algorand account needs 0.1 ALGO to exist and another 0.1 to hold an asset, and only the key holder can opt in — so nobody can do it for the agent, and USDC sent before the opt-in does not arrive, it fails. The 0.3 leaves something over for fees.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-muted max-w-2xl">
      <p><span class="font-medium text-base-content">Spending caps travel with the config either way:</span> <span class="font-mono">BOTTRUNK_MAX_PER_CALL</span> and <span class="font-mono">BOTTRUNK_MAX_PER_DAY</span>, in USDC. The server refuses anything above them before it signs. Set them: the catalog goes up to hundreds of dollars a call.</p>
      <p><%= link_to "Package and options on npm →", "https://www.npmjs.com/package/bottrunk-mcp", class: "underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-base-content" %></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="hairline">

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
    <h2 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight">Then: point your client at it</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-2xl">Every snippet below comes from that client's own documentation. Same server, same tools, twelve different ways of spelling it.</p>
    <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-3" aria-label="Clients">
      <% Docs::McpClient.all.each do |client| %>
        <a href="#<%= client.slug %>" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline border-base-300 text-muted hover:text-base-content rounded-full h-[34px] px-3.5 font-medium"><%= client.name %></a>
      <% end %>
    </nav>
  </section>

  <div id="clients" class="flex flex-col gap-14">
    <% Docs::McpClient.all.each do |client| %>
      <section id="<%= client.slug %>" class="flex flex-col gap-3.5 scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <h3 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-tight"><%= client.name %></h3>
          <span class="text-[13px] text-muted"><%= client.kind %></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted"><%= inline_code(client.summary) %></p>
        <%= render Docs::CodeSnippetComponent.new(snippets: { client.label => client.snippet }) %>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted"><%= inline_code(client.note) %> <%= link_to "#{client.name} docs →", client.docs_url, class: "underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-base-content", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %></p>
      </section>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <hr class="hairline">

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 lg:gap-20 items-start">
    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3.5">
      <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-tight">Something else?</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">Anything that speaks MCP over stdio works: the command is always <span class="font-mono">npx -y bottrunk-mcp</span>. And nothing forces you to use MCP at all — the endpoints are plain HTTP with an x402 paywall, so curl, Python or TypeScript work the same way.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">Running somewhere that can't spawn a local command — ChatGPT, Claude on the web, a hosted agent platform? Add <span class="font-mono">https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp</span> as a custom connector for discovery, and let the agent pay the endpoint directly with its own wallet.</p>
      <div class="mt-2"><%= render(Ui::ButtonComponent.new(href: "/docs", icon: "arrow-right")) { "Read the protocol docs" } %></div>
    </section>
    <section class="flex flex-col gap-3.5">
      <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-tight">What your agent gets</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">From the local package: <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_catalog</span> to see what exists and what it costs, <span class="font-mono">bottrunk_wallet</span> for the balance and today's spend, plus one paid tool per live service. From the remote endpoint: the same catalog, live quotes and order status, without a wallet. Ask it "what can you buy on BotTrunk?" to start.</p>
      <div class="mt-2"><%= render(Ui::ButtonComponent.new(href: root_path, icon: "arrow-right")) { "See the catalog" } %></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
